let's talk about gerrymandering if there  was only one way to repair the state of  representative government in the United  States it would be the elimination of  gerrymandering gerrymandering is a major  hindrance to the functioning of American  democracy  it's never the most discussed issue  because it's not an especially flashy or  exciting topic it's kind of dry it can  be complex you don't often see rallies  against gerrymandering but it's a really  really important problem and it gets  worse every decade the word  gerrymandering was first used in 1812  when the Massachusetts state legislature  redrew state Senate election lines to  favor the Democratic Republican Party  the bill was signed by Massachusetts  governor Elbridge Gerry a Boston  Magazine said one of the districts  looked like a salamander and the hens  referred to this practice as  gerrymandering the u.s. is split up into  435 congressional districts each getting  their own US House representative  federal election law requires districts  to be contiguous not separated by other  districts and the US Constitution  requires that each district have about  the same population so each  congressional district currently has  about 600,000 to 800,000 people living  in it every 10 years occasionally more  often congressional lines are drawn  based on the most recent census  population data to establish the  geography of each district states like  Alaska Delaware Montana North Dakota  South Dakota Vermont and Wyoming are  made up of a single statewide  congressional district because of their  low populations but all other states  have multiple districts and their  respective state legislatures are  responsible for drawing those lines  every individual state also has state  legislative districts each legislative  district within a state elects their own  representative to the state legislature  state legislatures are also responsible  for drawing the state-level legislative  lines whichever party has the majority  in a state legislature be it Democrats  or Republicans usually tries to draw  lines to better suit  spreading their party's voters into more  districts to sway election outcomes  this happens with both federal  congressional districts and state-level  legislative districts within a state  lawmakers get to choose voters instead  of it being the other way around  this is gerrymandering a clear conflict  of interest to serve partisan gain let's  say we have a state where 60% of the  population are blue party voters and 40%  are red party voters and this is a map  of where all the voters live in the  state based on this state's population  let's suppose there must be five  districts if you draw the district lines  straight down vertically like an example  one you get perfect representation the  districts are compact and the number of  blue and red districts are proportionate  to how many blue and red voters there  are in the state so they're likely to  elect three representatives from the  blue party and two representatives from  the red party but an example to where  the district lines are drawn  horizontally the districts are drawn  compactly but you get five blue  districts and all of the red party  voters are disenfranchised example three  is the most like what we have in reality  in a lot of states these districts here  aren't compact they're strategically  drawn into weird shapes and they don't  proportionately resemble the state's  makeup of voter party affiliation you  get more red districts than blue  districts this results in many  legislative and congressional districts  being drawn into strange shapes where  you can clearly tell the lines were  drawn in a strategic way a real-world  example is Illinois's 4th congressional  district this district is also known as  the Latin earmuffs because it connects  to largely Latino areas by a tiny thin  line running down a highway where no one  lives as a result Latinos who are  predominantly Democrats are all shoved  into one district which reduces the  influence that they could have in other  surrounding districts giving Republicans  disproportionate power both Democrats  and Republicans have been guilty of  gerrymandering but a 2014 analysis by  The Washington Post found that 8  the 10 most gerrymandered congressional  districts in the US were drawn by  Republicans and most congressional  gerrymandering in general is by  Republicans gerrymandering by  Republicans after the 2010 census has  put Democrats at a considerable  disadvantage on the national stage and  this is partially why Republicans have a  majority in both houses of Congress  after Barack Obama's 2008 victory  Republicans led an aggressive  state-by-state effort over the next few  years to gain most of the control over  redistricting in 2011 consequently in  the 2012 elections Wisconsin voted for  Obama for president they voted for a  Democratic senator and they cast more  votes for Democrats in their House races  yet thanks to Republican gerrymandering  they elected five Republicans and three  Democrats to the House of  Representatives and maintained a  Republican majority in both state  legislature houses Pennsylvania also  voted to reelect Obama voted for a  Democratic senator and cast more votes  for Democrats running for the house but  thanks to a Republican gerrymandering  the year before they elected 13  Republicans and five Democrats to the  House of Representatives here is what  Pennsylvania's seventh congressional  district looks like which reelected  Republican congressman Pat Mian  ridiculous same thing in Ohio Ohio voted  for Obama elected a Democratic senator  casted out half of its votes for  Democrats running for the house but due  to gerrymandering they elected 12  Republicans and four Democrats to the  house here is oh hi O's first  congressional district that reelected  Republican Steve Chabot to the house  because of Republican victories in state  government elections in 2010 many of  which they won because of gerrymandering  to begin with they were then able to  redraw congressional lines in states  with 40% of US House seats Democrats  redistricted states with only 10% of the  seats and the rest were drawn by divided  state governments nationwide in 2012  Democrats received 1.1 million more  votes than Republicans and House  elections but Republicans won 33 more  seats than Democrats  early does the party that wins the  popular vote for the House of  Representatives not take control of it  and it happened in 2012 in large part  due to gerrymandering on average only  about 10 to 15% of Americans approve of  Congress and how well they do their job  and yet in 2016 only 8 out of the 435  incumbents up for re-election in the US  House of Representatives were defeated  and replaced and the average margin of  victory across all House elections was  37.1% a huge amount a margin of victory  that we often see in countries with  government corruption and only 17 of the  435 seats were won by a margin of 5% or  less only 33 seats were won with a  margin of 10% or less that is to say in  2016 more than 9 out of 10 House races  were landslides where we pretty much  knew who was going to win before anyone  even voted only eight states had House  races that were even remotely  competitive something is wrong with this  if there's one thing that both Democrats  and Republicans should be able to agree  on it's this a 2013 poll conducted by  the Harris poll found that 71% of  Americans across all ideological and  party lines believe that those who stand  to benefit from redrawing congressional  districts should not have a say in how  they are redrawn so what is the solution  well we need independent nonpartisan or  bipartisan non-government Commission's  that draw district lines but it's the  state lawmakers who benefit from  gerrymandering who refuse to pass  legislation that would establish these  Commission's people need to communicate  to their state legislatures that they  are dissatisfied with lawmakers being  able to draw their own district lines we  should also elect democratic state  legislators or at least state  legislators who are willing to reform  redistricting processes but Democrats  are historically more open to  districting fairness and the idea of  allowing independent Commission's to  draw lines nothing will ever change if  the same people who have kept us in this  system are kept in power at the state  level former attorney General Eric  Holder recently signed on as a chairman  of the National Democratic  districting committee not-for-profit  organization that launched in January  the group focuses nationally on  redistricting reform to combat  Republican gerrymandering Barack Obama  has said that he too will be involved  with the organization over the next five  years the NDRC will be fighting battles  in court to make sure that Republicans  aren't violating redistricting laws  they'll be helping the campaign's of  state-level Democratic candidates that  could influence redistricting and  they'll be pushing for state referendums  that would allow voters to approve  redistricting reforms there are a lot of  other private nonprofit state-level  initiatives across the country pushing  for line-drawing to be done by  bipartisan non-governmental commissions  and those organizations need people  support the US Supreme Court will also  rule on partisan gerrymandering this  year a case that could considerably  change the processes of redistricting  throughout the country and even despite  Trump's appointment of Neel Gorsuch to  the Supreme Court there is hope because  conservative justices John Roberts and  Anthony Kennedy might vote with the  courts liberal minority against the  gerrymandering given their records on  similar matters gerrymandering is not  the most frequent issue that legislators  hear from their constituents about make  it an important issue call up your  congressional representative in your  state senator let them know that you  care about redistricting this is most  fundamental as an element of  representative government  tell them you want sensible reform maybe  offer to do something for a non-profit  in your state whose mission it is to  fight gerrymandering I promise you that  one exists when gerrymandering occurs  and the contest for a certain seat in  the US House or a state legislature is  not competitive that incumbent does not  need to earn your vote he or she doesn't  have as much incentive to hear your  voice and govern according to your  interests we're now only a few years  away from our next census in 2020 and  redistricting will follow soon  thereafter in 2021 now is the time to  take action on this issue if you don't  like politicians being able to  manipulate democracy like this
 

